.TH FSCK 1
.SH NAME
fsck \- perform file system consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfsck\fR [\fB\-aclmrsny\fR]\fR [\fIdevice\fR] ...\fR
.br
.de FL
.TP
\\fB\\$1\\fR
\\$2
..
.de EX
.TP 20
\\fB\\$1\\fR
# \\$2
..
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 5
.B \-n
For MFS4, verify the volume without writing any blocks. A pending committed
journal transaction is reported and is not replayed.
.TP 5
.B \-y
For MFS4, replay a fully validated committed journal transaction and apply
all supported deterministic repairs. This may free allocated data blocks
that are not referenced by any valid inode, so make a filesystem image backup
before using this option.
.TP 5
.B \-a
For MFS4, replay a validated committed journal transaction and apply only
safe repairs. Unreferenced allocated data blocks are preserved.
.TP 5
.B \-p
For MFS4, perform the same safe, preen-style repairs as
.BR \-a .
.TP 5
.B \-c
# Check and list only the specified i-nodes
.TP 5
.B \-l
# List the files and directories in the filesytem
.TP 5
.B \-r
# Prompt user for repairs if inconsistencies are found
.TP 5
.B \-s
# List the superblock of the file system
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP 20
.B fsck /dev/c0d0p3
# Check file system on \fI/dev/c0d0p3\fR
.TP 20
.B fsck \-a /dev/at0
# Automatically fix errors on \fI/dev/at0\fR
.TP 20
.B fsck \-l /dev/fd0
# List the contents of \fI/dev/fd0\fR
.TP 20
.B fsck \-c 2 3 /dev/c0d0p2
# Check and list \fI/dev/c0d0p2\fR i-nodes 2 & 3
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fIFsck\fR performs consistency checks on the file systems which reside 
on the specified devices.
When either the \fB\-a\fR or \fB\-r\fR flags are given, the file system
will be repaired if errors are found.
Before running \fIfsck\fR on a mounted file system, it must first be unmounted.
Trying to repair a mounted file system is dangerous and should not be 
attempted.
.PP
To repair the root file system (which cannot be unmounted), first 
type CTRL-F9 at the console to kill any and all processes.  Log back in
as \fBroot\fR, type \fIsync\fR to force any buffered changes to disk,
run \fIfsck\fR on the root file system and immediately reboot the
computer by typing \fIreboot\fR.
.PP
It is necessary to kill all processes before repairing the root file system
to prevent them from modifying any disk blocks while \fIfsck\fR is running.
This is only necessary for the root file system, any other file system can
simply be unmounted before it is checked.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkfs (1),
.BR mount (1).
.\" disk name refs corrected, i.e., old hd1 now c0d0p0 -- ASW 2005-01-15

.SH MFS4 VOLUMES
The MFS4 checker is selected by the on-disk magic number. The legacy MFS2/MFS3
checker remains unchanged for legacy volumes.
.PP
For MFS4, the default operation and
.B \-n
are read-only checks. The checker validates both superblock copies, the fixed
redo journal, allocation bitmaps, inode records, direct and indirect block
trees (including triple indirection), directory entries, orphan state,
extended-attribute chains, POSIX ACL values, link counts, and allocation
references.
.PP
The
.B \-a
and
.B \-p
options replay a journal transaction only after its commit record, header,
descriptors, and every metadata image have passed CRC and range validation.
They then repair deterministic metadata redundancies: restore a missing
redundant superblock from the valid peer, restore reserved and tail bitmap
bits, reconcile inode allocation bits with valid inode records, allocate
bitmap bits for referenced blocks, reconcile link counts where the directory
graph is unambiguous, and make inode mode bits agree with a valid access ACL.
They do not free unreferenced allocated data blocks.
.PP
The
.B \-y
option performs the same repairs and additionally clears allocation bits for
blocks that are not referenced by any valid inode, indirect tree, or xattr
chain. This may discard recoverable data from a lost allocation, so it must
not be used without a prior image backup.
.PP
After repairs, the checker flushes home metadata, rereads the repaired
superblocks and allocation maps, and reruns the consistency scan. It sets the
clean state only if the full scan succeeds and the orphan list is empty.
A structurally valid non-empty orphan list is left dirty for the MFS writable
mount recovery path.
.PP
The checker never guesses repairs for invalid block pointers, duplicate block
references, damaged directory entries, invalid orphan chains, malformed
xattr TLVs, or invalid ACL payloads. A committed journal whose header or
images fail validation is not discarded. Unknown incompat and read-only
compatible features are not repaired or replayed. Structural errors are
reported without changing home metadata.
.PP
Before running a repair, unmount the filesystem and make a backup image.
.B \-n
never writes blocks or replays the journal. Legacy selection and interactive
repair flags are not supported for MFS4.
